#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b85acdfc-0505-5472-b5f3-53270bffa030" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Parametrization of a Surface of Revolution: Rotation About the y-Axis or z-Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15270347-47c4-561d-9ad2-26e40f018f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386436} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab4ea2-ba5d-509f-9cfe-538e900a2dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386437} {:answer/id #uuid "4e439e3f-52f9-5cb6-ba09-c3ef4f9c226e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386438} {:answer/id #uuid "db3ac811-06b6-5e51-82be-efa7502d6c22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386439} {:answer/id #uuid "d682d175-341f-57ba-86ea-fc836551d1b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi {e}^{1-{y}^{2}}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15270347-47c4-561d-9ad2-26e40f018f22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386436} :answer-field/id #uuid "59451c4b-a0b1-5cc0-acc7-2f83bdb44fed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1753cbcf-7a39-5ffc-b095-2d1dbe00ebf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-109772" :question/problem "$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,\\text{_______}⟩\n$$\n\nThe vector-valued function $r(y,\\theta )$ above parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ What is the third component of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14081523/assets/be6fb27c0d050a7007e9f4a0d526daaff40b8f7d01f20c63ac8cc00074121f28.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $x=f(y)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta =[0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $x=f(y)={e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(y,\\theta )=⟨{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,{e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,y\\in [\\frac{1}{2},2],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the third component of $r$ is ${e}^{1-{y}^{2}}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e04ca999-94c1-5282-aaff-0fec4907f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386443} {:answer/id #uuid "2904168a-ba31-5c26-aa08-c443d608290a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386444} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4d0d42-c231-5a5c-a37a-6cce84458b05" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386445} {:answer/id #uuid "2a2fb7cf-e366-58bc-b198-46c488bdb079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386446} {:answer/id #uuid "0d9601e5-110b-549d-ab05-a125f89626e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e04ca999-94c1-5282-aaff-0fec4907f6cd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386443} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae5299b4-d083-5428-af6c-49ba0d98e811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "db97065a-3cdb-5549-bd2c-02e265017089" :question/math-academy-id "q-161051" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is given above. What is the $2$nd component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ and \n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\pi ,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{f}^{′}f{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-{f}^{′}f,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y),\\,(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386448 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4df896f9-7094-5d49-a5cf-3d0f62164298" :knowledge-point/title "The Fundamental Vector Product of a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81bdad5e-02ed-597d-8fd8-2d85f99d9d02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386450} {:answer/id #uuid "3b2e1a77-7061-5e89-af8c-a1518a16d464" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(x-2)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386451} {:answer/id #uuid "4ac15304-09b6-5a8a-a1f2-8d00e9fff9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386452} {:answer/id #uuid "1537d164-1275-5311-acad-4862b6c10b9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386453} {:answer/id #uuid "294d8e3c-d9a9-54be-a081-b3898adf03f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ac15304-09b6-5a8a-a1f2-8d00e9fff9c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-2)^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386452} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc7315b1-5f59-5ee1-9775-bdddd0e4dd75" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f5b32561-6d1f-5c43-8768-8280dac3008a" :question/math-academy-id "q-161076" :question/problem "$$\n⟨\\,\\text{_______}\\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\n$$\n\n \n\nThe fundamental vector product of the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=(x-2)^{2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [0,2]$ is given above. What is the $1$st  component of the product?" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y=(x-2)^{2}\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=(x-2)^{2},$ and  \n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f=(x-2)^{2},$ we obtain ${f}^{′}=2(x-2).$ So, finally, we conclude that the fundamental vector product is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}=⟨2(x-2)^{3},\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-(x-2)^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f311c0c-673e-5070-aef2-681f37a265b1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386457} {:answer/id #uuid "4f821d65-c81d-510b-ad1a-c90751dbc862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}\\sqrt{2+{y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386458} {:answer/id #uuid "145f6f8e-05fa-5252-a261-ebf41c4a8630" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)^{2}\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386459} {:answer/id #uuid "f7d6887d-546c-56d4-a57e-74f324df871b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+3{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386460} {:answer/id #uuid "a3894b19-8037-5a12-ae27-65db54521548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3894b19-8037-5a12-ae27-65db54521548" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386461} :answer-field/id #uuid "f67d93d0-e28c-55bc-a399-fe0827a893f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0abbafda-b663-58bc-a5e8-afa7f578ba96" :question/math-academy-id "q-161091" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $x={y}^{3}+1$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis for $y\\in [0,1].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $x={y}^{3}+1\\ge 0$ for all $y\\in [0,1].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨f(y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y,\\,f(y)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(y,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ and\n\n$$\nD={(y,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂y}\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂y}⟩=⟨{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & 1 \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & 0\\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f{f}^{′},\\,f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f{f}^{′})^{2}+(f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}+{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(y)={y}^{3}+1,$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(y)=3{y}^{2}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+(3{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{3}+1)\\sqrt{1+9{y}^{4}}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d7d1683-4735-5c93-9ad1-542e987aed1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Surface of Revolution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9f7ae40-d0d5-5ea6-b954-41a2138962d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386464} {:answer/id #uuid "c136c629-f46b-5610-b0db-226d3157e67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386465} {:answer/id #uuid "40ed605c-c9e4-5c36-9533-76d125067174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+(x-1)^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386466} {:answer/id #uuid "37284bdb-b12a-5208-9014-6137201f0f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x+1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386467} {:answer/id #uuid "511f5a1c-4a7d-59e4-85b2-28ed08fb413a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c136c629-f46b-5610-b0db-226d3157e67a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386465} :answer-field/id #uuid "181d256b-38ab-56b2-bb10-0a66cbbd1c13" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2efe31b-1093-57d0-b47a-05cf6c39fe86" :question/math-academy-id "q-161094" :question/problem "Use a fundamental vector product to determine which of the following integrals gives the surface area generated when the curve $y={e}^{x}(x-1)$ is rotated by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [2,3].$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that $y={e}^{x}(x-1)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in [2,3].$ The parametrization of our surface of revolution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,(x,\\theta )\\in D\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ and\n\n$$\nD={(x,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂x}⟩=⟨1,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,{f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\ {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta },\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂\\theta }⟩=⟨0,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =⟨|\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,-|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|,\\,|\\begin{aligned}1 & {f}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 0 & -f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}|⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +f{f}^{′}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨f{f}^{′},\\,-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖ & =\\sqrt{(f{f}^{′})^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(-f\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{f}^{2}({f}^{′})^{2}+{f}^{2}} \\\\  & =f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}‖{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{\\theta }^{′}‖\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta \\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\cdot \\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)={e}^{x}(x-1),$ we obtain ${f}^{′}(x)=x{e}^{x}.$ So, finally, we conclude that our expression for the surface area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}f\\sqrt{1+({f}^{′})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+(x{e}^{x})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{3}{e}^{x}(x-1)\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}{e}^{2x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
